/// A user's Google Workspace for an assistant, as native tools, each answered
/// by `api-request` calls against the `oauth_provider` the instance names,
/// stamped with the calling conversation's owner and compacted to what a model
/// needs. The user's token never reaches this component; `api-request`
/// attaches it host-side. Every call is nested under a request: an instance
/// whose clauses say `user = "*"` cannot call on its own account, so a caller
/// with no user of its own is answered `denied`.
///
/// Every capability is load-bearing: `config` names the provider and its
/// hosts, without which no call can be addressed; `store` carries each
/// request's state between the activation that issues its nested call and the
/// one that receives the answer; `tools` is the authority to call
/// `api-request`; `provides` is what makes the tools exist; `log` records each
/// step's tool, status and size — never a body, a header or a query.
///
/// The instance's `config = { ... };` map is flat: `provider` is the
/// `oauth_provider` name; `gmail_host` is the `host[:port]` Gmail is called on,
/// which `api-request` matches against that provider's `api_hosts` and the
/// instance's clauses must admit. A missing or malformed key fails every
/// activation, naming the key.
///
/// Size the instance's result inbox with a `channel at
/// "brenn:tool-results/<slug>"` block: each live request has at most one
/// nested call outstanding, so the window must hold Σ max_concurrency results,
/// twice over for a re-delivered result — `retain_depth ≥ 2 × Σ`, rounded up to
/// a power of two. 64 is the documented value: it holds any Σ max_concurrency
/// up to the 32 ceiling twice over, so tools joining this class change nothing
/// a deployed inbox needs, and the family default of 16 is too small for a full
/// window.
///
/// Every nested answer crosses the bus as a tool result, so Google's body — a
/// JSON string inside `api-request`'s value, inside the result envelope — must
/// fit `[messaging] max_body_bytes` (64 KiB default). Either raise that cap to
/// carry the OAuth provider's `max_response_bytes` (256 KiB default) with a
/// margin for escaping, `messaging { max_body_bytes = 393216; }`, or lower
/// `max_response_bytes` to fit the default cap, at the cost of most HTML mail.
/// Half again covers message bodies, which are base64url and do not grow when
/// escaped; sender-written headers dense in quotes or backslashes escape to
/// about twice their size, so such a read can pass the cap below the read
/// limit.
/// An answer over the bus cap never reaches this component as Google's answer.
component GoogleWorkspaceTools {
  abi = processor;
  requires = [log, store, config, tools, provides];
  in tool-results;
  in tool-requests;
  provide gmail-labels {
    mcp_name = "GmailLabels";
    description = "The user's Gmail labels. Without `id`, every label with its `id`, `name` and `type` (`system` or `user`), in Google's order; Google gives no counts in a listing. With `id` (a label's `id`, such as `INBOX` or `Label_12`), that one label with its `messages_total`, `messages_unread`, `threads_total` and `threads_unread`, each when Google gives it. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail and Calendar endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history, Calendar) are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320 } }, "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
  provide gmail-search {
    mcp_name = "GmailSearch";
    description = "Search the user's Gmail with Gmail's search syntax (`q`, as in Gmail's search box: `from:alice@example.com is:unread newer_than:7d`). Answers `messages`, newest first as Google orders them, one line per message: `id`, `thread_id`, `from`, `subject` and `date` (the header values as sent, each when present; a value over 1000 bytes (UTF-8) is cut, ends in `…`, and the line's `headers_truncated` lists the fields cut), `internal_date` (when Gmail received it, RFC 3339 UTC), `snippet` (Gmail's preview of the text) and `labels` (user labels by name, system labels such as `INBOX` and `UNREAD` by id); `result_size_estimate` (Google's estimate of all matches); `next_page` when there are more — pass it back as `page` with the same `q`; and `unavailable`, the ids of listed messages deleted before they could be read. `max` is how many messages to return, 1 to 25, default 10; each costs one call to Google. `spam_trash: true` searches spam and trash too. Bodies are not included. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail read scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail and Calendar endpoints these tools do not cover (attachments, profile, history, Calendar) are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "q": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 1024 }, "max": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 25 }, "page": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 4096 }, "spam_trash": { "type": "boolean" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320 } }, "required": ["q"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    max_concurrency = 4;
  }
}
